module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
import sysl.text.str_builder

// The instruction loop: one `Op` after another, and the sentence a thrown value carries.
//
// **Cut out of `execute.sysl`, which keeps the entry points and the calls.** It reaches back for
// `pop`, `peek`, `arrange_named` and `invoke`, each of which says so where it is declared, and
// `guarded` reaches in here for `run_frames`. **`lay_frame` and `lay_bindings` went back there** when
// this file passed a thousand lines in its turn: laying a call's slots out before the first
// instruction runs is arranging a call, which is that file's subject.
//
// **`run_compose.sysl` IS THE SECOND CUT, AND IT COULD ONLY BE MADE ACROSS THE `match`.** This file
// is one function whose body is one arm-list over `Op`, so nothing stands outside that list to move:
// what went instead is the twelve arms that touch none of the loop's own state -- `MakeArray`,
// `MakeObject`, `MakeRange`, `Join`, `Repeat`, `WithObject`, `WithMerge`, `JoinArrays`, `SetIndex`,
// `SetField`, `IterInit` and `IterAwaitCheck`. Each reads and writes the operand stack and nothing
// else, so each is expressible as a function; an arm that enters a frame writes `pc`, `base`,
// `code`, `chunk_ix`, `given` and `vm.scope` together and could not have gone.
//
// **THE ARMS THAT STAYED ARE THE CHEAP ONES, AND THAT IS THE RULE FOR THE NEXT CUT TOO.** A call
// reaching a lifted arm is a fraction of an allocation, a list walk or a lookup, and nothing about
// `PushInt`, `LoadSlot`, `Jump` or `Pop` would survive being one.
// `bench/results/2026-09-20-run-frames-split.md` carries the measurement that says the cut is timed
// neutral.
//
// **THE OPERATORS ARE THE ONE PLACE THAT RULE IS READ THE OTHER WAY, and `run_arith.sysl` says so
// at length.** There is one arm per operator here -- `Add`, `Less`, `ShiftLeft` and the rest -- and
// each is a pop, a pop and a call to a leaf with exactly one caller. Writing the twenty integer
// fast paths out in the arms is what a reader would want and takes this file two hundred lines past
// a thousand, which is the rule that decided it.

// The sentence a thrown value carries -- what an uncaught fault SAYS, where the value itself is what
// a `catch` is handed.
//
// **A caught fault re-thrown keeps its own words**, which is what `throw e` inside a `catch` is for:
// a library that decides not to handle something puts it back rather than replacing it with a
// sentence of its own. That is why an object with a `message` is read for that field rather than
// rendered -- rendering would turn the fault into `{message: "...", line: 3, file: "..."}`, which is
// a description of a fault rather than the fault.
//
// `thrown_message` and `thrown_payload` -- the two halves of what a thrown value means -- are
// `runtime.sysl`'s, beside the `Fail` they fill: `reject` and `fail` raise a fault the same way from
// a promise, and a second copy of this rule would be a second answer to `is a string a sentence`.

// `throw v` as the error channel wants it.
//
// **A STRING IS A MESSAGE FAULT AND EVERYTHING ELSE CARRIES ITS VALUE**, which is the whole of the
// rule and is what keeps every `catch e -> e.message` written before this true: a thrown string has
// nothing in it a handler could sort on, so it is flattened to a sentence exactly as it always was,
// and the `catch` is handed the `{message, line, file}` object `fault_value` builds. Anything else --
// a data variant, a class instance, a number -- travels whole, so `catch NotFound(id)` can ask what
// went wrong rather than reading a sentence.
//
// **The LOCATION the fault is REPORTED at becomes the `throw`'s own** and does not travel, a `Fail`
// carrying a span into a source that a caught fault no longer holds. The value's own fields are
// untouched, so a fault object put back with `throw e` still remembers where it was raised.
private raised(at: Span, v: Value) -> Step = v match
    Str(p) -> fault(at, p.s)
    other -> Err(value_failure(at, thrown_message(at, other), other))

// What a name nothing has bound is reported as.
//
// **Where the name is one the ENTRY file's top level binds, the sentence says where the value
// belongs.** An actor's VM runs every module the program imports and does not run the entry file's
// top level, so a handler naming a `val` written there finds nothing -- and *"is not defined"* on its
// own sends the reader to look for a spelling mistake in a line that is right. `Vm.entry_effects`
// holds those names and is filled only where a VM was loaded for an actor, so an ordinary program's
// faults say exactly what they always said.
//
// **The JavaScript back end says the same words**, in `$.caught` in `js_rt_pat.sysl`: a program that
// prints `e.message` from a `catch` must read alike on both.
//
// Not `private`: `run_compose.sysl`'s `compose_check_named` says the same sentence about an
// annotated binding whose name is gone.
unbound_here(name: string) -> string
    val known = current().entry_effects

    for i in 0..<known.len()
        if known.at(i) == name
            return s"`${name}` is not defined -- the entry file's top level is not run inside an actor, and `${name}` is bound there. Put it in a module the actor imports, or in the actor's own fields."

    s"`${name}` is not defined"
end unbound_here

// The instruction loop.
//
// **The instruction pointer and the scope are sysl locals rather than fields of the top frame**, and
// written back only when a call pushes or pops one -- or when an `await` suspends. Reading them out of
// a `Buf` on every instruction would copy a whole frame per step.
//
// **`entering` says whether this is the START of a call rather than the resumption of one**, which
// is the difference between a slot window that has to be laid and one that is already standing.
// **It is said rather than worked out from `from == 0`**, which was the same question by proxy and
// stopped being one when a generator arrived: `start_generator` lays its slots and parks the machine
// before a single instruction runs, so the first `gen_step` comes back here with `pc == 0` and its
// arguments already in place.
//
// Not `private`: reached from `execute.sysl`.
run_frames(u: *Unit, start: usize, from: usize, base0: usize, floor: usize, outer_scope: *EnvObj,
           entering: bool, args: Buf[Value], given0: usize) -> Step
    // **The machine this loop runs, taken once.** It is a field of the VM (`vm_state.sysl`) rather
    // than module storage, and a pointer to it is what keeps every `vm.stack` below written the way
    // it always was -- the accessor is paid for once per call to `run_frames` and not once per
    // instruction.
    val vm = machine()

    // **The VM itself, taken once for the same reason, and it is handed DOWN rather than asked for
    // again.** `current()` compiles to a thread-local lookup -- a `_tlv_get_addr` call on this
    // platform, which nothing optimises away -- so every helper this loop reaches that asks for the
    // VM itself pays one per instruction. The ones that are hot take it as a parameter instead:
    // `pop_on`/`peek_on` (the machine), `keepable_on` and `maybe_collect_on` (the VM), and the fused
    // arms. Each keeps a `current()`-asking form beside it for the cold callers that have nothing to
    // hand down.
    val here = current()

    // **Whether to count instructions, decided once for the whole of this loop.**
    // `CountingInstructions` is a build-time constant, so a default build folds this to `false` and
    // the optimiser deletes the branch below along with it -- which is what makes the profiler cost
    // nothing rather than nearly nothing. It was measured both ways and `profile.sysl` carries the
    // numbers: the branch is 2.4% of the geometric mean of `bench/` and 8% of the tightest loop, and
    // the folded build is 0.999.
    //
    // **Nothing may turn the instrument on while a chunk is running**, which is what makes reading it
    // once correct: `main.sysl` sets it before `run` and clears it after, and a nested `run_frames` --
    // a builtin running a callback -- reads it again on the way in.
    val profiling_here = profile_on && CountingInstructions

    var chunk_ix = start

    // **THE CHUNK IS READ ONCE HERE, WHERE IT STANDS, AND ONCE PER CALL, AND NEVER ON A RETURN** --
    // `Buf.at` would copy the whole `Chunk` for two fields. What the loop carries on with comes from
    // the plan that chose a call and from the frame at a return.
    // **The machine keeps `current().current_source` in step with the chunk it is running**, which is
    // what lets `failure` stamp a fault with the file its span belongs to. It is set here and at every
    // point below that changes `chunk_ix`; there are three, and they are the only three.
    val entered = &u.chunks.elems[chunk_ix]

    here.current_source = entered.source
    var pc = from
    var base = base0
    var code = code_view(entered.code)

    // **A chunk entered at its head lays its slots and one resumed does not.** The cells sit at
    // `base` and upward, below anything the body computes, so `Ret`'s truncate back to `base` is what
    // frees them and the collector walks them as part of the stack it already walks. A coroutine
    // comes back here with its slots still standing on the machine's own stack.
    //
    // **Which parameters arrived**, one bit each -- the answer `JumpIfGiven` wants. It is a local
    // beside `pc` and `base` for their reason: reading it out of the top frame on every default
    // would copy a frame per step.
    var given = if entering then lay_frame(u, chunk_ix, args) else given0

    // **Where the last counted instruction stood**, which is what tells the profiler that this one
    // ran directly after it rather than being arrived at by a call, a return, a jump or a resumed
    // coroutine. A position alone is not enough -- a return lands one past its call -- so the frame's
    // own `base` is carried with it, and a callee's base is never its caller's. These are read and
    // written only under `profiling_here`, so a default build folds every use away with the branch.
    var counted_pc = pc
    var counted_base = base
    var counted_any = false

    loop
        // **Every chunk the compiler makes ends in `Ret`**, so running off the end is a defect in
        // the compiler; it is stopped here, where the unchecked read below would otherwise go on.
        if pc >= code.len then panic("slate ran past the end of a chunk's instructions")

        // Unchecked, and the test above is the check: `pc` is below the count here.
        val ins = code.ins[pc]
        val at = ins.at
        val here_pc = pc

        pc += 1

        // **One branch on a local, and that is the whole cost of the profiler when it is off.**
        // `profile.sysl` says why the instrument is shaped this way and
        // `bench/results/2026-09-18-profiler-instrumentation-cost.md` carries the measurement that
        // says this costs nothing a benchmark can see.
        if profiling_here
            profile_step(ins.op, counted_any && here_pc == counted_pc + 1 && base == counted_base)
            counted_pc = here_pc + op_width(ins.op) - 1
            counted_base = base
            counted_any = true

        // **THE ORDER THE ARMS ARE WRITTEN IN IS LOAD-BEARING: ONLY THE FIRST FORTY-TWO OF THEM GET
        // THE SINGLE-LEVEL JUMP TABLE.** The back end folds this arm list into two switches and puts
        // exactly forty-two arms in the first, whichever ones they are; the other sixty-eight share a
        // SECOND table reached through the first's default, which is nine more instructions and a
        // second unpredictable indirect branch on every one of them. So the list is in two blocks --
        // the instructions programs actually execute, then everything else -- and the boundary is the
        // comment below rather than a convention anybody has to remember.
        //
        // **`Op`'s DECLARATION ORDER IS THE OTHER HALF OF IT.** A table is built only where the tags
        // it spans are dense enough, so the hot instructions have to hold near-neighbouring tags as
        // well as near-neighbouring arms -- which is why `Tick` and the fused instructions are
        // declared among the early variants in `code.sysl` rather than at the end where they were
        // added. `bench/results/2026-09-22-one-jump-table.md` reads both tables out of the binary and
        // has the measurement.
        ins.op match
            PushInt(v) -> vm.stack.push(Int(v))
            PushReal(v) -> vm.stack.push(Real(v))
            PushStr(k) -> vm.stack.push(str_counted(u.strings.at(k), u.string_chars.at(k)))

            LoadSlot(i) -> vm.stack.push(vm.stack.at(base + i))
            DeclareSlot(i) ->
                val v = pop_on(vm)

                keepable_on(here, v, at, "bound to a local")?
                vm.stack.set(base + i, v)
            StoreSlot(i) ->
                val v = pop_on(vm)

                keepable_on(here, v, at, "written to a local")?
                vm.stack.set(base + i, v)

            // A read the compiler resolved to one of its file's own definitions, which is an array
            // read where `LoadName` hashes a spelling and walks the chain outward. The hoisted
            // definition filled the cell before any statement of the file ran, so it is there.
            LoadDef(d) -> vm.stack.push(here.defs.at(d))

            // The same table for a `val` or a `var` the file wrote at its own top level, which is
            // bound when its statement is reached rather than hoisted -- so an empty cell is a read
            // that has run too early and takes the walk it would have taken.
            LoadCell(d, k) ->
                val cell = if usize(d) < here.defs.len() then here.defs.at(usize(d)) else Undefined

                cell match
                    Undefined -> compose_load_cell(u, vm, usize(k), at)?
                    v -> vm.stack.push(v)
            StoreCell(d, k) -> compose_store_cell(u, vm, here, usize(d), usize(k), at)?

            LoadName(k) ->
                val name = u.strings.at(k)

                lookup(vm.scope, name) match
                    Some(v) -> vm.stack.push(v)
                    None -> return fault(at, unbound_here(name))

            // **ONE ARM PER OPERATOR, AND NOTHING BELOW THEM ASKS WHICH ONE IT WAS.** The token used
            // to ride on the instruction and be matched three more times on the way to the answer;
            // the emitter picks the arm now and `run_arith.sysl` holds the twenty bodies, each a
            // pair of machine integers in front of the general arithmetic.
            Add ->
                val b = pop_on(vm)
                val a = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(added(a, b, at)?)
            Sub ->
                val b = pop_on(vm)
                val a = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(subtracted(a, b, at)?)
            Mul ->
                val b = pop_on(vm)
                val a = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(multiplied(a, b, at)?)
            Div ->
                val b = pop_on(vm)
                val a = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(divided(a, b, at)?)
            IntDiv ->
                val b = pop_on(vm)
                val a = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(quotient_of(a, b, at)?)
            Rem ->
                val b = pop_on(vm)
                val a = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(remainder_of(a, b, at)?)
            Less ->
                val b = pop_on(vm)
                val a = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(is_less(a, b, at)?)
            LessEq ->
                val b = pop_on(vm)
                val a = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(is_less_eq(a, b, at)?)
            Greater ->
                val b = pop_on(vm)
                val a = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(is_greater(a, b, at)?)
            GreaterEq ->
                val b = pop_on(vm)
                val a = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(is_greater_eq(a, b, at)?)
            Equal ->
                val b = pop_on(vm)
                val a = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(is_equal(a, b, at)?)
            NotEqual ->
                val b = pop_on(vm)
                val a = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(is_unequal(a, b, at)?)

            MakeObject(n) -> compose_object(vm, n, at)?

            GetIndex ->
                val k = pop_on(vm)
                val target = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.push(read_at(target, k, at)?)
            // **THE SUBJECT STAYS ON THE STACK ACROSS THE READ**, because a `get` property runs
            // slate code and a `Value` in a sysl local is not a root. Popping first and then calling
            // a getter that allocates is exactly the shape `A `Value` IN A SYSL LOCAL IS NOT A ROOT`
            // describes, and the cost of not doing it is one truncate.
            GetField(k, c) ->
                val got = read_field_site(peek_on(vm, 0), u, usize(k), usize(c), at)?

                vm.stack.truncate(vm.stack.len() - 1)
                vm.stack.push(got)
            SetField(k, c) -> write_named_site(vm, here, u, usize(k), usize(c), at)?

            Jump(to) -> pc = to
            JumpIfFalse(to) -> if !pop_on(vm).truthy() then pc = to

            // The same question asked of a BINDING SITE's own mask, which stands on the stack rather
            // than in the frame -- so a binding inside a binding carries its own and nothing has to
            // be saved or put back.
            JumpIfSet(n, to) -> peek_on(vm, 0) match
                Int(m) -> if (usize(m) >> usize(n)) & 1 == 1 then pc = usize(to)
                _ -> ()

            Pop -> vm.stack.truncate(vm.stack.len() - 1)
            Discard ->
                peek_on(vm, 0) match
                    Promise(pr) ->
                        pr.orphaned = true
                        orphan_failed(pr)
                    _ -> ()

                vm.stack.truncate(vm.stack.len() - 1)

            UnpackSlots(w, masked) -> compose_unpack_slots(u, vm, base, w, masked, at)?

            // A binding whose pattern is a row of bare names, which is what a `for` head nearly
            // always is. The matcher is not entered at all: the cells are written out of the subject
            // and the sentence a subject that does not fit is owed is the matcher's own.
            UnpackFixed(w, object, masked) ->
                val wide = u.wide.at(usize(w))
                val pat = u.patterns.at(wide.a)

                if !unpacked_fixed(vm, pat, object, masked, base + wide.b, wide.c)
                    return fault(at, unpack_complaint(pat, peek_on(vm, 0)))

            TestSlots(k, first) -> compose_test_slots(u, vm, base, usize(k), usize(first))

            IterNext(to) ->
                val i = peek_on(vm, 0) match
                    Int(n) -> usize(n)
                    _ -> 0

                // **Where the counter goes next, which is one on for everything but a table.** A
                // removed entry stays in place so that the positions in the index do not shift, so
                // a walk over a set or a map skips it and lands past it.
                var ahead = i + 1

                val step = peek_on(vm, 1) match
                    Array(p) ->
                        if i >= p.items.len() then None else Some(p.items.at(i))
                    Bytes(p) ->
                        if i >= p.data.len() then None else Some(Int(long(p.data.at(i))))
                    Str(p) ->
                        if i >= str_count(p) then None else Some(str_counted(str_char_text(p, i), 1))
                    RangeVal(lo, hi, inclusive, _, _, step) ->
                        if i >= range_count(lo, hi, inclusive, step) then None else Some(Int(range_at(lo, step, i)))

                    // **A set yields its members and a map its pairs**, which is what JavaScript's
                    // `for...of` does with each -- so `for (k, v) in m` is an array pattern over
                    // the pair, needing nothing of its own.
                    SetVal(t) ->
                        var j = i

                        while j < t.entries.len() && t.entries.at(j).dead
                            j += 1

                        ahead = j + 1

                        if j >= t.entries.len() then None else Some(t.entries.at(j).key)
                    MapVal(t) ->
                        var j = i

                        while j < t.entries.len() && t.entries.at(j).dead
                            j += 1

                        ahead = j + 1

                        if j >= t.entries.len()
                            None
                        else
                            val e = t.entries.at(j)

                            var pair: Buf[Value] = buf()

                            pair.push(e.key)
                            pair.push(e.value)
                            Some(Array(new_array(pair)))

                    // **A generator ignores the counter and is asked instead.** The loop keeps
                    // counting beside it, which costs an integer and keeps this instruction one
                    // shape; what says whether there is another value is the generator, and only it
                    // can know. This runs slate code from inside the instruction loop, which is what
                    // a native calling back already does.
                    Gen(id, gen) -> gen_iter_next(id, gen, at)?
                    _ -> return fault(at, "`for` walks an array, bytes, a string, a range, a generator, a set or a map")

                step match
                    None -> pc = to
                    Some(v) ->
                        vm.stack.set(vm.stack.len() - 1, Int(long(ahead)))
                        vm.stack.push(v)

            // **THE SAFE POINT.** It stands in front of every statement outside a loop and once at
            // the top of every loop, where it used to stand in front of every statement full stop:
            // a loop's body gives its statements' up to the loop's own, that being the one place a
            // statement runs more than once for having been written once. `safe_point` in
            // `emit.sysl` is where that is decided.
            Tick ->
                maybe_collect_on(here)

                // A full heap is reported where there is a line to point at; the allocator has no
                // idea which statement it was serving.
                if here.heap_exhausted then return fault(at, "this program has run out of memory")

            // **THE FUSED PAIRS, AND THE `pc += 1` IN EACH IS WHAT STEPS OVER THE SLOT `fuse` LEFT
            // STANDING.** The emitter overwrites only the first instruction of a pair, so the second
            // is still in the code buffer for anything that jumps at it -- and an arm that forgot to
            // skip it would run the tail twice. **Each fast path is a CALL and is meant to be**, this
            // loop having no registers to spare for it; `run_fused.sysl` holds what each one does and
            // says what inlining them cost, and `code.sysl` says why each.
            LoadSlot2(i, j) ->
                fused_load_slot2(vm, base, usize(i), usize(j))
                pc += 1
            LoadSlotInt(i, v) ->
                fused_load_slot_int(vm, base, usize(i), long(v))
                pc += 1
            AddStoreSlot(i) ->
                // `code.ins[pc]` is the `StoreSlot` that was stepped over, read for its span alone
                // and only where the sum is not a whole number -- which is the one path whose
                // sentence belongs to the store rather than to the addition.
                fused_add_store_slot(vm, base, i, at)? match
                    None -> ()
                    Some(v) -> stored_in_slot(vm, base, i, v, code.ins[pc].at)?

                pc += 1
            LessJumpIfFalse(to) -> if fused_less_jump_if_false(vm, at)? then pc = to else pc += 1
            SubStoreSlot(i) ->
                fused_sub_store_slot(vm, base, i, at)? match
                    None -> ()
                    Some(v) -> stored_in_slot(vm, base, i, v, code.at(pc).at)?

                pc += 1

            // **THE FUSED RUNS.** A head steps over four slots or leaves by the exit its
            // `JumpIfFalse` holds; a tail always leaves by its `Jump`. A slot stepped over is read
            // only for the span a slow path owes, or for the exit when the loop ends.
            TickSlotIntLess(i, v) ->
                val goes = fused_tick_slot_int_less(here, vm, base, usize(i), long(v), at)? match
                    Some(b) -> b
                    None -> fused_slot_less(vm, base, usize(i), long(v), code.at(pc + 2).at)?

                if goes then pc += 4 else pc = fused_exit(code.at(pc + 3).op)
            SlotIntAddStoreJump(i, v, to) ->
                if !fused_slot_int_add(vm, base, usize(i), long(v))
                    fused_slot_int_add_slow(vm, base, usize(i), long(v), code.at(pc + 1).at, code.at(pc + 2).at)?

                pc = usize(to)

            CallFn(packed_argc, packed_names) ->
                val argc = usize(packed_argc)
                val names = usize(packed_names)

                val callee = peek_on(vm, argc)

                // **THE ORDINARY CALL LEAVES ITS ARGUMENTS WHERE THEY STAND.** They are already at
                // the position the callee's slots want them in, so the only thing between here and
                // a frame is the callee sitting underneath them -- and `placed_call` is what says
                // whether anything else has to look at them first. **A BUILTIN LEAVES THEM STANDING
                // TOO**, there being no frame to lay: it reads them through a window onto the stack
                // and they come off with the callee once it has answered.
                placed_call(u, callee, names, vm.frames.len()) match
                    InPlace(body, scope, params, slots, entering_code, entering_source) ->
                        for i in 0..<argc
                            keepable_on(here, peek_on(vm, argc - 1 - i), at, "passed to a function")?

                        // **The callee comes out from under its own arguments**, which is the whole
                        // of the move: one shift down of `argc` cells against a malloc, a free and
                        // two copies of every argument. A slate value is plain storage -- it holds
                        // nothing reference counted, which `obj.sysl` states as a rule -- so moving
                        // one within the stack is a copy and no more.
                        val top = vm.stack.len()
                        val start = top - argc - 1

                        for i in 0..<argc
                            vm.stack.set(start + i, vm.stack.at(start + i + 1))

                        vm.stack.truncate(top - 1)

                        val mask = lay_standing(vm, params, slots, argc)

                        // The frame this call is leaving is written back only here, out of locals,
                        // which is what makes the ordinary instruction cheap.
                        vm.frames.push(Frame(chunk_ix, pc, vm.scope, base, vm.handlers.len(), given, code, here.current_source))
                        chunk_ix = body
                        code = entering_code
                        here.current_source = entering_source
                        pc = 0
                        vm.scope = scope
                        base = start
                        given = mask
                    Builtin(nf) -> vm.stack.push(standing_native(nf, argc, false, at)?)
                    Buffered ->
                        here.args_buffered += 1

                        var written: Buf[Value] = buf()

                        for i in 0..<argc
                            keepable_on(here, peek_on(vm, argc - 1 - i), at, "passed to a function")?
                            written.push(peek_on(vm, argc - 1 - i))

                        vm.stack.truncate(vm.stack.len() - argc - 1)

                        val args = if names == 0 then written else arrange_named(u, callee, written, u.arg_names.at(names), 0, at)?

                        invoke(u, callee, args, at, vm.frames.len())? match
                            Answered(v) -> vm.stack.push(v)
                            Enter(body, called, laid, entering_code, entering_source) ->
                                // **The arguments go down FIRST, before anything else in this arm.**
                                // They are a sysl local until they are on the stack, and the mark
                                // phase cannot see one -- so the fewer instructions between `invoke`
                                // releasing its hold and this, the less there is to go wrong when one
                                // of them later learns to allocate.
                                val laid_base = vm.stack.len()
                                val mask = lay_frame(u, body, laid)

                                vm.frames.push(Frame(chunk_ix, pc, vm.scope, base, vm.handlers.len(), given, code, here.current_source))
                                chunk_ix = body
                                code = entering_code
                                here.current_source = entering_source
                                pc = 0
                                vm.scope = called
                                base = laid_base
                                given = mask

            // `o.m(a)`, which is where the receiver rule lives.
            //
            // **The lookup and the call are one instruction because the rule needs both.** By the
            // time a `GetField` has handed a function to a `CallFn`, the fact that decides whether
            // an object is passed -- was it the object's own, or a shared one reached through a
            // proto -- has been thrown away.
            CallMethod(packed_argc, w) ->
                // **The one hot instruction that reads `Unit.wide`**, and it reads it once for the
                // three operands it cannot carry: the method's name, the written argument names and
                // this site's cache. `code.sysl` says why a call can afford the load.
                val wide = u.wide.at(usize(w))
                val argc = usize(packed_argc)
                val k = wide.a
                val names = wide.b
                val c = wide.c

                val name = u.strings.at(k)
                val target = peek_on(vm, argc)

                // **A builtin type answers a method by TABLE and an object answers by field.** An
                // object is not in the table at all -- its names are the program's, and `proto` is
                // already the mechanism by which one gets methods. `method.sysl` says why.
                val looked = target match
                    Object(fields) -> field_from_site(fields, k, at, u, c)?
                    other -> builtin_from_site(other, name, at, u, c)?

                // **The five universal methods are what an object falls back to**, after its own
                // fields and its whole proto chain have answered nothing. A builtin kind has already
                // had the same fallback inside `builtin_receiver`.
                // **A class or a data name answers the three shape methods here**, where its own
                // fields and its proto chain have said nothing -- so a static the program declared
                // wins over them.
                val found = if is_absent(looked.0) then shape_receiver(target, name) match
                    Some(pair) -> pair
                    None -> universal_receiver(name) match
                        Some(pair) -> pair
                        None -> looked
                else looked

                // **THE ORDINARY METHOD CALL LEAVES ITS ARGUMENTS WHERE THEY STAND TOO, and a
                // DELEGATED one does not even move the receiver.** The target sits directly under
                // the written arguments, which is exactly the order a delegated method's parameters
                // are in -- so parameter zero is already at `base` and there is nothing to do. A
                // method the object carries itself takes no receiver, so the target comes out from
                // under its arguments the way `CallFn`'s callee does.
                //
                // **A BUILTIN METHOD READS THE SAME WINDOW**, `found.1` saying whether the receiver
                // is inside it: every method of a builtin kind is the free function of that name with
                // the receiver as its first argument.
                placed_call(u, found.0, names, vm.frames.len()) match
                    InPlace(body, scope, params, slots, entering_code, entering_source) ->
                        for i in 0..<argc
                            keepable_on(here, peek_on(vm, argc - 1 - i), at, "passed to a function")?

                        val top = vm.stack.len()
                        val start = top - argc - 1

                        var laid = argc + 1

                        if !found.1
                            for i in 0..<argc
                                vm.stack.set(start + i, vm.stack.at(start + i + 1))

                            vm.stack.truncate(top - 1)
                            laid = argc

                        val mask = lay_standing(vm, params, slots, laid)

                        vm.frames.push(Frame(chunk_ix, pc, vm.scope, base, vm.handlers.len(), given, code, here.current_source))
                        chunk_ix = body
                        code = entering_code
                        here.current_source = entering_source
                        pc = 0
                        vm.scope = scope
                        base = start
                        given = mask
                    Builtin(nf) -> vm.stack.push(standing_native(nf, argc, found.1, at)?)
                    Buffered ->
                        here.args_buffered += 1

                        var args: Buf[Value] = buf()

                        // **The object goes in front of the written arguments where it was delegated
                        // to.** A method the object carries itself has already captured what it
                        // needs; a shared one cannot have, there being one of it for every instance.
                        // A builtin method is delegated to by the same rule -- it is the free
                        // function of that name, and the receiver is its first argument.
                        if found.1 then args.push(target)

                        for i in 0..<argc
                            keepable_on(here, peek_on(vm, argc - 1 - i), at, "passed to a function")?
                            args.push(peek_on(vm, argc - 1 - i))

                        vm.stack.truncate(vm.stack.len() - argc - 1)

                        if is_absent(found.0)
                            // **A PROPERTY IS NOT A METHOD, AND THE SENTENCE SAYS SO RATHER THAN
                            // THAT THE NAME IS MISSING.** `c.area()` reads the property and then
                            // calls whatever it answered; left to the ordinary complaint the reader
                            // is told `area` is not a field of an object that plainly has one.
                            target match
                                Object(o) ->
                                    if has_property(o, name, at)?
                                        return fault(at, s"`${name}` is a property, not a method -- it is read as `.${name}`, with no brackets after it")
                                _ -> ()

                            return fault(at, s"`${name}` is not a field of this object, so there is nothing to call")

                        // **The receiver is parameter zero and is already in the list**, so the
                        // names cover what was written and the arrangement is told how many slots
                        // are spoken for.
                        val placed = if names == 0 then args else arrange_named(u, found.0, args, u.arg_names.at(names), if found.1 then 1 else 0, at)?

                        invoke(u, found.0, placed, at, vm.frames.len())? match
                            Answered(v) -> vm.stack.push(v)
                            Enter(body, called, laid, entering_code, entering_source) ->
                                // **The arguments go down FIRST, before anything else in this arm.**
                                // They are a sysl local until they are on the stack, and the mark
                                // phase cannot see one -- so the fewer instructions between `invoke`
                                // releasing its hold and this, the less there is to go wrong when one
                                // of them later learns to allocate.
                                val laid_base = vm.stack.len()
                                val mask = lay_frame(u, body, laid)

                                vm.frames.push(Frame(chunk_ix, pc, vm.scope, base, vm.handlers.len(), given, code, here.current_source))
                                chunk_ix = body
                                code = entering_code
                                here.current_source = entering_source
                                pc = 0
                                vm.scope = called
                                base = laid_base
                                given = mask

            Ret ->
                val v = pop_on(vm)

                vm.stack.truncate(base)

                if vm.frames.len() == floor
                    vm.scope = outer_scope
                    return Ok(v)

                val back = vm.frames.at(vm.frames.len() - 1)

                vm.frames.truncate(vm.frames.len() - 1)

                // **Anything the returning call was inside goes with it.** A `return` written in a
                // `try` body skips its `PopHandler`, and a handler outliving the frame it was
                // established in names an instruction nobody is standing at.
                // **A RETURN READS NO CHUNK AT ALL** -- the frame carries its own code and file.
                vm.handlers.truncate(back.handlers)
                chunk_ix = back.chunk
                code = back.code
                here.current_source = back.source
                pc = back.pc
                vm.scope = back.scope
                base = back.base
                given = back.given
                vm.stack.push(v)

            // `o.m(...xs)`. The receiver rule is `CallMethod`'s and is not restated: what differs is
            // that the arguments arrive as an array, and the receiver goes in front of them where
            // the method was delegated to.
            CallMethodSpread(k) ->
                val name = u.strings.at(k)
                val spread = pop_on(vm)
                val target = peek_on(vm, 0)

                val looked = target match
                    Object(fields) -> field_from(fields, name, at)?
                    other -> builtin_receiver(target, name, at)?

                // **The five universal methods are what an object falls back to**, after its own
                // fields and its whole proto chain have answered nothing. A builtin kind has already
                // had the same fallback inside `builtin_receiver`.
                // **A class or a data name answers the three shape methods here**, where its own
                // fields and its proto chain have said nothing -- so a static the program declared
                // wins over them.
                val found = if is_absent(looked.0) then shape_receiver(target, name) match
                    Some(pair) -> pair
                    None -> universal_receiver(name) match
                        Some(pair) -> pair
                        None -> looked
                else looked

                here.args_buffered += 1

                var args: Buf[Value] = buf()

                if found.1 then args.push(target)

                spread match
                    Array(a) ->
                        for i in 0..<a.items.len()
                            keepable_on(here, a.items.at(i), at, "passed to a function")?
                            args.push(a.items.at(i))
                    _ -> return fault(at, "unreachable")

                vm.stack.truncate(vm.stack.len() - 1)

                if is_absent(found.0)
                    // **A PROPERTY IS NOT A METHOD, AND THE SENTENCE SAYS SO RATHER THAN THAT THE
                    // NAME IS MISSING.** `c.area()` reads the property and then calls whatever it
                    // answered; left to the ordinary complaint the reader is told `area` is not a
                    // field of an object that plainly has one.
                    target match
                        Object(o) ->
                            if has_property(o, name, at)?
                                return fault(at, s"`${name}` is a property, not a method -- it is read as `.${name}`, with no brackets after it")
                        _ -> ()

                    return fault(at, s"`${name}` is not a field of this object, so there is nothing to call")

                invoke(u, found.0, args, at, vm.frames.len())? match
                    Answered(v) -> vm.stack.push(v)
                    Enter(body, called, laid, entering_code, entering_source) ->
                        // **The arguments go down FIRST, before anything else in this arm.** They
                        // are a sysl local until they are on the stack, and the mark phase cannot
                        // see one -- so the fewer instructions between `invoke` releasing its hold
                        // and this, the less there is to go wrong when one of them later learns to
                        // allocate.
                        val laid_base = vm.stack.len()
                        val mask = lay_frame(u, body, laid)

                        vm.frames.push(Frame(chunk_ix, pc, vm.scope, base, vm.handlers.len(), given, code, here.current_source))
                        chunk_ix = body
                        code = entering_code
                        here.current_source = entering_source
                        pc = 0
                        vm.scope = called
                        base = laid_base
                        given = mask

            // `yield e` -- stand still and let whoever is driving take the value.
            //
            // **The frame is written back and the machine is NOT parked**, which is where this parts
            // company with `Await`. An `await` is resumed by the scheduler, so it has to be in
            // `parked` before it lets go; a generator is resumed by `gen_step`, which is the very
            // function this `Err(Wait)` is about to return to -- so parking is that function's move,
            // and doing it here as well would park twice and lose the machine.
            //
            // **The value stays on the stack for `Await`'s reason**: the machine's own stack is a
            // root the tracer walks, and the sysl local this arm could have kept it in is not.
            Op.Yield ->
                vm.frames.push(Frame(chunk_ix, pc, vm.scope, base, vm.handlers.len(), given, code, here.current_source))

                return Err(Wait)

            Op.Await ->
                // **A machine that is not a coroutine has nowhere to come back from.** The compiler
                // refuses `await` outside an `async` function, so this is only reachable if a chunk
                // were started the ordinary way -- and suspending the main program would leave `run`
                // with nothing to return.
                if vm.promise == null || floor != 0
                    return fault(at, "`await` cannot suspend this call")

                // **The awaited value stays on the stack while the machine is parked**, which is what
                // keeps a pending promise reachable: the shadow stack cannot hold it across the park,
                // and the machine's own operand stack is a root the tracer already walks. The answer
                // is written over it when the machine comes back.
                val awaited = peek_on(vm, 0)

                // What `resume_chunk` will pick the loop up from.
                vm.frames.push(Frame(chunk_ix, pc, vm.scope, base, vm.handlers.len(), given, code, here.current_source))

                val id = park()

                awaited match
                    Promise(pr) ->
                        pr.handled = true

                        // **A settled promise still goes round the queue.** Continuing here instead
                        // would make `await` on a finished promise run the rest of the function before
                        // anything else queued ahead of it -- which is the ordering bug every async
                        // runtime that took the shortcut has had to undo. JavaScript resolves this
                        // case through a microtask for the same reason.
                        if pr.settled
                            current().ready.push(Resumption(id, pr.value, pr.failed, pr.thrown, pr.message, pr.at, pr.src, pr.quit, pr.quit_status))
                        else
                            waiter_push(pr, id)
                    other ->
                        // `await 5` is 5, as in JavaScript -- and it still yields, so that a program
                        // cannot tell which of the two it was given by watching what runs next.
                        current().ready.push(Resumption(id, other, false, false, "", at, current().current_source, false, 0))

                return Err(Wait)

            // ---- THE COLD BLOCK. Since sysl 0.0.127 the whole `match` is ONE jump table over every
            // tag, so this line no longer decides a table; it is kept as where the instructions a
            // loop never runs are written, below the ones it does. ----

            SealData -> compose_seal_data(vm)
            PushModule(m) -> vm.stack.push(here.module_values.at(m))

            CheckType(w) -> compose_check_named(u, vm, w, at)?
            CheckResult(t, pk) -> compose_check_result(u, vm, usize(t), usize(pk), at)?

            DefineTest(k) -> here.registered_tests.push(TestCase(u.strings.at(k), here.current_source, pop_on(vm)))
            DefineHook(kind, k) -> set_hook(usize(kind), u.strings.at(usize(k)), pop_on(vm))

            PushHandler(to) -> vm.handlers.push(Handler(chunk_ix, to, vm.stack.len(), vm.frames.len(), vm.scope, base, given, false))
            PopHandler -> vm.handlers.truncate(vm.handlers.len() - 1)
            Raise -> return raised(at, pop_on(vm))

            WantDisposable -> want_disposable(peek_on(vm, 0), at)?
            PushDispose(to) -> vm.handlers.push(Handler(chunk_ix, to, vm.stack.len(), vm.frames.len(), vm.scope, base, given, true))

            // **`Ok` of nothing, because the answer is thrown away.** `guarded` runs a disposal
            // handler for its effect and then raises the signal it was already carrying.
            DisposeDone -> return Ok(Null)

            // The head of a chunk with default parameters, and nowhere else.
            JumpIfBound(k, to) -> if bound_here(vm.scope, u.strings.at(usize(k))) then pc = usize(to)

            // The same, where that chunk's parameters are slots. One bit rather than a lookup, which
            // is what the whole of this arrangement is.
            JumpIfGiven(n, to) -> if (given >> usize(n)) & 1 == 1 then pc = usize(to)

            PushNull -> vm.stack.push(Null)
            PushUndefined -> vm.stack.push(Undefined)
            PushBool(v) -> vm.stack.push(Bool(v))

            // **The magnitude is shared with the unit's copy rather than rebuilt**, a `BigInt`
            // copying with a retain and never being written through.
            PushBig(k) -> vm.stack.push(big_value(u.bigs.at(k)))
            PushShape(pk, k) -> vm.stack.push(shape_value(u.patterns.at(usize(pk)), u.strings.at(usize(k)), vm.scope))
            PushExternal(k) -> vm.stack.push(External(k))

            CheckSlot(i, w) -> compose_check_slot(u, vm, base, usize(i), w, at)?

            // The checker refuses this before the machine runs. It is here so that the one path where
            // it does not says what it has always said.
            ImmutableSlot(k) -> return fault(at, s"`${u.strings.at(k)}` is a `val`, so it is written once")

            DeclareCell(w, mutable) -> compose_declare_cell(u, vm, here, w, mutable, at)?
            DeclareVal(k) -> compose_declare_name(u, vm, here, k, false, at)?
            DeclareVar(k) -> compose_declare_name(u, vm, here, k, true, at)?
            DeclareAbsent(k) -> compose_declare_absent(u, vm, k)
            StoreName(k) -> compose_store_name(u, vm, here, k, at)?

            // The three prefix operators, and the bitwise ones no benchmark reaches.
            Negate -> vm.stack.push(negated(pop_on(vm), at)?)
            Not -> vm.stack.push(notted(pop_on(vm))?)
            Complement -> vm.stack.push(complemented(pop_on(vm), at)?)
            BitAnd -> compose_bit_and(vm, at)?
            BitOr -> compose_bit_or(vm, at)?
            BitXor -> compose_bit_xor(vm, at)?
            ShiftLeft -> compose_shift_left(vm, at)?
            ShiftRight -> compose_shift_right(vm, at)?
            NoOperator -> return fault(at, "this is not an arithmetic operator")

            MakeArray(n) -> compose_array(vm, n, at)?
            SetIndex -> write_indexed(vm, at)?

            JumpIfTrueKeep(to) -> if peek_on(vm, 0).truthy() then pc = to
            JumpIfPresentKeep(to) -> if !is_nullish(peek_on(vm, 0)) then pc = to
            JumpIfAbsentKeep(to) -> if is_nullish(peek_on(vm, 0)) then pc = to
            JumpIfFalseKeep(to) -> if !peek_on(vm, 0).truthy() then pc = to

            Dup -> vm.stack.push(peek_on(vm, 0))
            DupAt(n) -> vm.stack.push(peek_on(vm, n))
            RotUp(n) -> compose_rot_up(vm, n)

            Join(n) -> compose_join(vm, n, at)?
            Repeat -> compose_repeat(vm, at)?
            WithObject(n) -> compose_with_object(vm, n, at)?
            WithMerge(spread) -> compose_with_merge(vm, spread, at)?
            MakeRange(inclusive, has_lo, has_hi, has_step) ->
                compose_range(vm, inclusive, has_lo, has_hi, has_step, at)?
            Unpack(k, immutable) -> compose_unpack(u, vm, usize(k), immutable, at)?
            IsPat(k, negated) -> compose_is_pat(u, vm, usize(k), negated)

            PushScope -> vm.scope = new_env(vm.scope)
            PopScope -> vm.scope = vm.scope.parent

            MakeClosure(c) -> vm.stack.push(Fn(new_closure(u.chunks.at(c).params, c, vm.scope)))
            DefineFn(k, c) -> compose_define_fn(u, vm, usize(k), usize(c))

            // The same, and the closure also goes into the table a `LoadDef` reads. **The table grows
            // to reach the cell rather than being sized in advance**: a unit's definitions are
            // numbered across every file it holds, and a machine may run only some of them.
            DefineDef(w) -> compose_define_def(u, vm, w)

            TestPat(k) -> compose_test_pat(u, vm, k)

            IterInit -> compose_iter_init(vm, at)?
            IterAwaitCheck -> compose_iter_await_check(at)?

            FailWith(k) -> return fault(at, u.strings.at(k))
            FailShowing(k) -> return fault(at, u.strings.at(k) + show_value(peek_on(vm, 0)))
            FailNoArm -> return fault(at, s"no arm of this match applies to ${value_text(peek_on(vm, 0))}")

            // `f(...xs)` -- the arguments are an array the instruction above it built.
            //
            // **The only difference from `CallFn` is where the count comes from**, which is the
            // whole point: everything after that -- the keepable check, the truncate, `invoke` and
            // the two ways a call can go -- is the same code doing the same thing.
            CallSpread ->
                here.args_buffered += 1

                val spread = pop_on(vm)
                val callee = pop_on(vm)

                var args: Buf[Value] = buf()

                spread match
                    Array(a) ->
                        for i in 0..<a.items.len()
                            keepable_on(here, a.items.at(i), at, "passed to a function")?
                            args.push(a.items.at(i))
                    _ -> return fault(at, "unreachable")

                invoke(u, callee, args, at, vm.frames.len())? match
                    Answered(v) -> vm.stack.push(v)
                    Enter(body, called, laid, entering_code, entering_source) ->
                        // **The arguments go down FIRST, before anything else in this arm.** They
                        // are a sysl local until they are on the stack, and the mark phase cannot
                        // see one -- so the fewer instructions between `invoke` releasing its hold
                        // and this, the less there is to go wrong when one of them later learns to
                        // allocate.
                        val laid_base = vm.stack.len()
                        val mask = lay_frame(u, body, laid)

                        vm.frames.push(Frame(chunk_ix, pc, vm.scope, base, vm.handlers.len(), given, code, here.current_source))
                        chunk_ix = body
                        code = entering_code
                        here.current_source = entering_source
                        pc = 0
                        vm.scope = called
                        base = laid_base
                        given = mask

            // The top `n` arrays, made into one. What builds a spread argument list.
            JoinArrays(n) -> compose_joined_arrays(vm, n, at)?
end run_frames
